leetcode:-  148. Sort List

ques:-
Given the head of a linked list, return the list after sorting it in ascending order.
example:-  Input: head = [4,2,1,3]
Output: [1,2,3,4]


IMP:-

⭐ Short answer

We use fast = head->next so that:

✅ slow stops at end of first half
✅ list splits into two smaller halves every time
✅ prevents infinite recursion

🔥 Understand with smallest failing example

List:

1 → 2

❌ If fast = head
slow = 1
fast = 1


Loop condition:

fast && fast->next → true


Move:

slow = 2
fast = NULL


Now:

mid = slow->next = NULL
slow->next = NULL


So split becomes:

left = 1 → 2
right = NULL


👉 Left part is SAME as original list
👉 Recursion never shrinks
👉 Infinite recursion 💀

✅ If fast = head->next
slow = 1
fast = 2


Loop condition fails immediately

So:

mid = slow->next = 2
slow->next = NULL


Split:

left = 1
right = 2


👉 Perfect split
👉 Recursion shrinks
👉 Works correctly

and mid=slow->next  (point to then start of the right half)
approach:-

class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* merge(ListNode* l1,ListNode* l2){
        ListNode* dummy=new ListNode(0);
        ListNode* node=dummy;
        while(l1!=NULL && l2!=NULL){
            if(l1->val>l2->val){
                node->next=l2;
                l2=l2->next;
            }
            else{
                node->next=l1;
                l1=l1->next;
            }
            node=node->next;
        }
        if(l1!=NULL) node->next=l1;
        if(l2!=NULL) node->next=l2;

        return dummy->next;

    }
    ListNode* sortList(ListNode* head) {
        if(head==NULL || head->next==NULL) return head;
       ListNode* slow=head;
       ListNode* fast=head->next;
       while(fast!=NULL && fast->next!=NULL){
        slow=slow->next;
        fast=fast->next->next;
       }
       ListNode* mid=slow->next;
       slow->next=NULL;
       ListNode* left=sortList(head);
       ListNode* right=sortList(mid);
       return merge(left,right);
    }
};